}_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\n\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Dirichlet boundary conditions. So, setting ${\\alpha }_{i}=1,{\\beta }_{i}=0,a=0,$ and $b=\\pi ,$ the matrix $A$ reduces to\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0) & {y}_{2}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(\\pi ) & {y}_{2}(\\pi )\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we solve the auxiliary equation\n\n$$\n{m}^{2}+1=0,\n$$\n\nwhich has roots $\\pm i.$ Thus, the fundamental solutions are\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ and }{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =0(-1)-1(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)=0$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has infinitely many solutions.}$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-339097/field-1" "q-339097/field-2" "q-339097/field-3" "q-339097/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-339097"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-339176/field-1/eacec3da0d49aa56b1d7012ab9e3dec997b7d12cbbba21f6dc41f1863d280a9a" :answer/id #uuid "948bc0f8-f3af-5350-80bc-989074863ffe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :a