his BVP {{field-4}}." :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have the second-order homogeneous boundary value (BVP) problem\n\n$$\n{y}^{″}(x)+P(x){y}^{′}(x)+Q(x)y=0,\\,a\\le x\\le b\n$$\n\nwith the Robin (mixed) boundary conditions\n\n$$\n{\\alpha }_{1}y(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}^{′}(a)=0,\\,{\\alpha }_{2}y(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}^{′}(b)=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ and $Q$ are continuous on $[a,b],$ and ${\\alpha }_{i}$ and ${\\beta }_{i}$ are real constants such that $({\\alpha }_{i},{\\beta }_{i})\\ne (0,0).$ Furthermore, suppose the fundamental solutions to the equation are ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x).$ \n\nNow, let's define the following matrix:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\,{\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{1}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{1}^{′}(a) & {\\alpha }_{1}{y}_{2}(a)+{\\beta }_{1}{y}_{2}^{′}(a) \\\\ {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{1}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{1}^{′}(b) & {\\alpha }_{2}{y}_{2}(b)+{\\beta }_{2}{y}_{2}^{′}(b)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThen, the BVP has a unique, trivial solution $y≡0$ if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$\nIn other words, if the determinant equals zero, then the BVP has *infinitely many solutions*.\n\nNotice that, in our case, we have Robin boundary conditions, given by\n\n$$\ny(0)+{y}^{′}(0)=0,\\,y(1)-{y}^{′}(1)=0.\n$$\n\nSo, setting ${\\alpha }_{1}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{1}=1,$ ${\\alpha }_{2}=1,$ ${\\beta }_{2}=-1,$ and $a=0,$  $b=1,$ the matrix $A$ becomes\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}(0)+{y}_{1}^{′}(0) & {y}_{2}(0)+{y}_{2}^{′}(0) \\\\ {y}_{1}(1)-{y}_{1}^{′}(1) & {y}_{2}(1)-{y}_{2}^{′}(1)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the fundamental solutions, we set $y={e}^{mx}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-3m+2 & =0 \\\\ (m-1)(m-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have ${m}_{1}=1$ and ${m}_{2}=2,$ and so the fundamental solutions are \n\n$$\n{y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}\\,\\text{and}\\,{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n{y}_{1}^{′}(x)={e}^{x},\\,{y}_{2}^{′}(x)=2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the matrix $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{0}+{e}^{0} & {e}^{3(0)}+2{e}^{2(0)} \\\\ {e}^{1}-{e}^{1} & {e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{2(1)}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =2(-{e}^{2})-0(3) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $det(A)\\ne 0,$ we conclude that this BVP $\\text{has a unique solution }y≡0.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-338980/field-1" "q-338980/field-2" "q-338980/field-3" "q-338980/field-4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d9576e0-3be5-5d05-8cc8-c3ecde496b46"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-338980"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186228435 :answer-field/correct 17592186228436 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186229667 :question/answer-fields 17592186228420] {:db/id "q-338525/field-2/ma-v1/461f7cbfc6a1960526370073a405c9aaa6391e98ee7bf22921bfc65c0e2d84c2/985badd296b7c6c04e58719e043d43ea3f095cf16a5d52fc7c81ad0484c967e0" :answer/id #uuid "3ee0444d-1993-5b9e-8160-1b5a71a856b5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-2x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338525/field-2/ma-v1/461f7cbfc6a1960526370073a405c9aaa6391e98ee7bf22921bfc65c0e2d84c2/c2065921b07b139a65bbe0758034f94e72be70c66b1193cdf77a73cad8a53e50" :answer/id #uuid "b4298bd8-b56a-5198-b598-9ff14d04590b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ and ${y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338525/field-2/ma-v1/461f7cbfc6a1960526370073a405c9aaa6391e98ee7bf22921bfc65c0e2d84c2/5f6a04ebb2b04dacf40c11bd5b14b2febbc6ed33d2b0a71cbe4e1ba655bd4b1f" :answer/id #uuid "9eb6bc51-a778-51ac-a016-3ffe06a42290" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{3x}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={e}^{-x}$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-338525/field-2/ma-v1/461f7cbfc6a1960526370073a405c9aaa6